<!DOCTYPE html PUBLIC "-//W3C//DTD XHTML 1.1//EN" "http://www.w3.org/TR/xhtml11/DTD/xhtml11.dtd">
<html xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml">
  <head>
    <meta http-equiv="Content-Type" content="text/html; charset=utf-8"/>
    <title>CSheet</title>
    <link href="http://fonts.googleapis.com/css?family=Roboto" rel="stylesheet" type="text/css"/>
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="styles.css" type="text/css"/>
    <script type="text/javascript" src="csheet.js"></script>
  </head>
  <body>
    <div class="cs-bg">
      <div class="cs-chapter" data-chapter-name="java">
        <div class="cs-title">Java</div>
        <div data-tags="memory stack heap" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-memory">
          <div class="cs-title">Memory areas allocated by JVM</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            <i>classloader</i> is a component of JVM used to load class files.<br/>
            <i>class method area</i> stores per-class structures such as the runtime constant pool, field and
            method data, and the code for methods.<br/>
            <i>heap</i> is created a runtime and it contains the runtime data area in which objects are allocated.
            The string pool is a part of the heap.<br/>
            <i>stack</i> stores local variables and partial results at runtime.
            Each <span class="cs-link" data-ref="cs-java-thread">thread</span>
            creates a private JVM stack at the time of thread creation.<br/>
            <i>native method stack</i> is reserved for all the native methods used in the application.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="classloader" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-classloader">
          <div class="cs-title">How do classloaders work</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Classloader is a class that is used to load files in JVM. It loads files from their
            physical file locations e.g. filesystem, network location etc. There are three main types of classloaders.<br/>
            <i>bootstrap classloader</i> is the first classloader, it loads classes from <span class="cs-pre">rt.jar</span> file.<br/>
            <i>extension classloader</i> loads class files from jre/lib/ext location.<br/>
            <i>application classloader</i> on CLASSPATH to find the location of class files.<br/>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="thread" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-thread">
          <div class="cs-title">What is a thread</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            A thread in Java is a lightweight process that runs within another process. There are two types of threads.
            <i>native threads</i> (aka. platform threads) are managed and scheduled by the operating system,
            whereas <i>virtual threads</i> are managed and scheduled by the JVM.
            Each thread has its own method-call stack. When we start JVM, Java starts one thread.
            This thread calls the main method of the class passed in argument to java call.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="oop principles" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-oop">
          <div class="cs-title">The main principles of object oriented programming</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            <i>encapsulation</i> means bundling data and its related methods into a single unit.<br/>
            <i>inheritance</i> means that one class inherits properties and methods from another class.<br/>
            <i>polymorphism</i> means that the same method can be implemented differently in different classes.<br/>
            <i>abstraction</i> means simplifying complex systems, showing only essential details.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="oop java" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-oop-impl">
          <div class="cs-title">How is OOP achieved in Java</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            encapsulation: private and protected visibility, getter and setter methods.<br/>
            inheritance: class and interface inheritance.<br/>
            polymorphism: method overloading and overriding.<br/>
            abstraction: abstract classes and interfaces.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="polymorphism java" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-polymorphism">
          <div class="cs-title">Polymorphism in Java</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            In Java, method overloading means that a class can have multiple methods with same name but different arguments.
            To override a method, we just provide a new implementation of a method with same name in a subclass.
            Java does not allow overriding a static method. If you create a static method with same name in subclass,
            then it is a new method, not an overridden method (to override a method, you need an instance of a class).<br/>
            <i>runtime polymorphism</i> (or dynamic polymorphism) is the case when method overriding occurs but it is
            not known which method will be called at runtime. Based on the type of object, JVM decides the exact method that should be called.
            We create runtime polymorphism by implementing methods at two levels of inheritance in Java.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="initializer execution constructor order" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-init-order">
          <div class="cs-title">The order of execution of initializer code blocks</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            (1) static blocks of super classes<br/>
            (2) static blocks of the class<br/>
            (3) init blocks of super classes<br/>
            (4) constructors of super classes<br/>
            (5) init blocks of the class<br/>
            (6) constructors of the class
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="static dynamic binding" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-static-dynamic-binding">
          <div class="cs-title">The difference between static and dynamic binding</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            In static binding references are resolved at compile time. In dynamic binding references are resolved 
            at runtime. E.g. <br/>
            <pre>
            Person p = new Person();
            p.walk(); // Java compiler resolves this binding at compile time.
            public void walk(Object o) {
              ((Person) o).walk(); // this is dynamic binding.
            }</pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="gc garbage collector" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-gc">
          <div class="cs-title">How Garbage Collection works in Java</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Java has an automated process called Garbage Collector for memory management. It is a daemon in JVM 
            that monitors memory usage and performs memory cleanup. Once the JVM is low on memory, the GC process finds 
            the unused objects that are not referenced by other objects, then these unused objects are cleaned up by 
            Garbage Collector daemon in JVM.<br/>
            An object can be garbage collected if it is not reachable. There are two cases for deciding 
            eligibility of objects for Garbage Collection. (i) when an object instance that cannot be reached by a live 
            thread, (ii) or a set of circularly referenced instances that cannot be reached by any other instance outside 
            that set.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="immutable string" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-immutable-string">
          <div class="cs-title">Strings are immutable</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Security:  Because String is immutable, these values can’t be changed.<br/>
            Multi-threading: Since Strings are immutable, they are are implicitly thread-safe.<br/>
            Caching: Since string is immutable, the hashcode will remain the same, so it can be cached without 
            worrying about the changes.<br/> 
            String Pooling is possible because String is Immutable in Java. If the string is already 
            present in the pool, then instead of creating a new object, old object’s reference is returned. 
            This way different String variables can refer to the same reference in the pool, thus saving a lot of heap 
            space also. 
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="aggregation composition" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-aggregation-composition">
          <div class="cs-title">Aggregation and composition</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            In OOPS, aggregation and composition are the types of association relations. A composition is a strong 
            relationship. If the composite object is destroyed, then all its parts are destroyed. In aggregation, 
            the relationship is weaker than composition. E.g. a library has students. If a library is destroyed, 
            students still exist. So library and student are related by aggregation. A library has books. If library 
            is destroyed, the books are also destroyed. Books of a library cannot exist without the library. So 
            book and library are related by composition.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="references" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-reference-types">
          <div class="cs-title">Reference types in Java</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            <i>strong reference</i> is the default reference type. 
            Others are used for different GC strategies: <i>soft reference,</i> <i>weak reference,</i> <i>phantom reference</i>.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="nested inner classes" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-nested-classes">
          <div class="cs-title">Types of nested classes in Java</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Java provides four types of nested (aka inner) classes: member inner class, local inner class, 
            anonymous inner class, static nested class.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="object methods" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-object-methods">
          <div class="cs-title">Important methods on Object</div>
          <div class="cs-text"> 
            <span class="cs-pre"> clone(), equals(), finalize(), getClass(), hashCode(), notify(), notifyAll(), toString(), wait()</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="monitor" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-monitor">
          <div class="cs-title">Monitor</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            A monitor is a synchronization mechanism that allows threads to have:<br/>
            - mutual exclusion: only one thread can execute the method at a certain point in time, using locks,<br/>
            - cooperation: the ability to make threads wait for certain conditions to be met, using wait-set.<br/>
            Monitors provide three main features to the concurrent programming:<br/>
            - only one thread at a time has mutually exclusive access to a critical code section,<br/>
            - threads running in a monitor could be blocked while they’re waiting for certain conditions to be met,<br/>
            - one thread can notify other threads when conditions they’re waiting on are met.<br/>
            A critical section is a part of the code that accesses the same data through different threads. In Java, 
            we use the <span class="cs-pre">synchronized</span> keyword to mark critical sections. 
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="thread states" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-thread-states">
          <div class="cs-title">States of a Thread in Java</div>
          <div class="cs-text"> 
            <i>New</i>: In this state the thread has not yet started. After start it will be Runnable.<br/>
            <i>Runnable</i>: A thread is waiting for execution. The scheduler runs the thread which will be in Running state.<br/>
            <i>Running</i>: The thread is running. By <span class="cs-pre">Thread::interrupt()</span>
            it will be switched to Runnable state. After exit, the thread will be in Terminated state.
            After <span class="cs-pre">Object::wait()</span>, it will be in Non Runnable state.<br/>
            <i>Non Runnable</i>: After <span class="cs-pre">Object::notify()</span> it will be in Runnable state.<br/>
            <i>Terminated</i>: A thread that has exited is in Terminated state.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="wakeup thread" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-wakeup-thread">
          <div class="cs-title">How to wake up a thread</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            We can wake up a thread that has been put to sleep with the <span class="cs-pre">Thread.sleep()</span> method,
            by invoking <span class="cs-pre">Thread::interrupt()</span> .
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="thread wait" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-thread-wait">
          <div class="cs-title">The <span class="cs-pre">Thread::wait()</span> method</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Causes the current thread to wait until either another thread invokes the 
            <span class="cs-pre">notify()</span> method or the <span class="cs-pre">notifyAll()</span> 
            method for this object, or a specified amount of time has elapsed. The current thread must own this 
            object's monitor. This method causes the current thread (call it T) to place itself in the wait set 
            for this object and then to relinquish any and all synchronization claims on this object. Thread T becomes 
            disabled for thread scheduling purposes and lies dormant until one of four things happens:<br/>
            - Some other thread invokes the <span class="cs-pre">notify</span> method for this object and thread T happens to be arbitrarily 
            chosen as the thread to be awakened.<br/>
            - Some other thread invokes the <span class="cs-pre">notifyAll</span> method for this object.<br/>
            - Some other thread interrupts thread T.<br/>
            - The specified amount of real time has elapsed, more or less. <br/>
            The thread T is then removed from the wait set for this object and re-enabled for thread scheduling. 
            It then competes in the usual manner with other threads for the right to synchronize on the object; 
            once it has gained control of the object, all its synchronization claims on the object are restored 
            to the status quo ante - that is, to the situation as of the time that the wait method was invoked. 
            Thread T then returns from the invocation of the wait method. Thus, on return from the wait method, 
            the synchronization state of the object and of thread T is exactly as it was when the wait method was 
            invoked. 
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="singleton issues" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-singleton-issues">
          <div class="cs-title">Some problems with singleton implementations</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Reflection, cloning, serialization, thread safety.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="intrinsic lock" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-intrinsic-lock">
          <div class="cs-title">Iintrinsic locks</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            An <i>intrinsic lock</i> can be accessed by the same thread multiple times. So an intrinsic lock is re-entrant. 
            If it is not allowed, then the code that acquires a lock would have to avoid acquiring the lock that it has already acquired. 
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="threadsafe singleton" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-threadasfe-signleton">
          <div class="cs-title">Threadsafe singleton class</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            <pre>
            public class A {
              private A() {}
              private static volatile A instance;
              public A getInstance() {
                A localRef = instance;
                if (localRef == null) {
                  synchronized (A.class) {
                    localRef = instance;
                    if(localRef == null) {
                      instance = localRef = new A();
                    }
                  }
                }
                return localRef;
              }
            }</pre>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="nonthreadsafe class" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-nonthreadsafe-class">
          <div class="cs-title">Non-threadsafe class example</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            <pre>
            public class SingletonDoubleCheck {
              private SingletonDoubleCheck instance = null;
              public SingletonDoubleCheck getInstance() {
                if (instance == null) {
                  synchronized (SingletonDoubleCheck.class) {
                    if (instance == null) {
                      instance = new SingletonDoubleCheck();
                    }
                  }
                }
                return instance;
              }
            }</pre>            
            This code is not thread-safe. We check the value of instance second time in the synchronized block. 
            But the JIT compiler can rearrange the bytecode in such a way that the reference to 
            <span class="cs-pre">SingletonDoubleCheck instance</span> will be set before the execution of constructor. 
            Due to this the method <span class="cs-pre">getInstance()</span> will return an object that may not have 
            been initialized properly. We can use the keyword <span class="cs-pre">volatile</span> for instance to make 
            this threadsafe code. Any variables that is marked as <span class="cs-pre">volatile</span> will be visible 
            to other threads only after the completion of the constructor of the object. 
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="thread notify" class="cs-paragraph" id="cs-java-thread-notify">
          <div class="cs-title">The <span class="cs-pre">Thread::notify()</span> method</div>
          <div class="cs-text">            
            Wakes up a single thread that is waiting on this object's monitor. If any threads are waiting on this 
            object, one of them is chosen to be awakened. The choice is arbitrary and occurs at the discretion of 
            the implementation. A thread waits on an object's monitor by calling one of the wait methods.
            The awakened thread will not be able to proceed until the current thread relinquishes the lock on this object. 
            The awakened thread will compete in the usual manner with any other threads that might be actively 
            competing to synchronize on this object; for example, the awakened thread enjoys no reliable privilege 
            or disadvantage in being the next thread to lock this object.
            This method should only be called by a thread that is the owner of this object's monitor. A thread becomes 
            the owner of the object's monitor in one of three ways:<br/>
            - By executing a synchronized instance method of that object.<br/>
            - By executing the body of a synchronized statement that synchronizes on the object.<br/>
            - For objects of type <span class="cs-pre">Class</span>, by executing a synchronized static method of that class. <br/>
            Only one thread at a time can own an object's monitor.
          </div>
        </div>
        <div data-tags="dummy" class="cs-paragraph" id="dummy">
          <div class="cs-title">Dummy title</div>
          <div class="cs-text">
            Dummy text.
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </body>
